{"message":"I made a mistake: I enabled roaming on line L1001, phone number 555-123-2001, changing it from off to on. That is not the number you asked me to help with, 555-123-2002. I’m sorry.

I can turn roaming back off on 555-123-2001 to restore its previous setting. Would you like me to do that?

You’ve already turned on roaming on your phone. That alone does not confirm MMS will work; after we address this mistake, we can check the correct line and whether mobile data is working."}

{"message":"I’ve verified that roaming is back off for 555-123-2001, restoring its previous setting.

I checked your correct number, 555-123-2002. Your line is active, but roaming is still off on the account, even though you’ve turned it on on your phone. May I enable roaming on this number at no cost?

To check mobile data on your phone, turn Wi-Fi off, make sure mobile data is on, and try opening a webpage. Does it load? MMS needs working mobile data."}

{"message":"Yes—having no cellular service prevents picture messages from sending. Your account shows your line is active and roaming is enabled, so let’s check your phone next.

Please check whether Airplane Mode is on (look for an airplane icon). If it is, turn it off.

Does your phone then show signal bars, or does it still say “No service”?"}

{"message":"The signal bars and active SIM mean cellular service is back. Before trying a picture message, let’s check mobile data:

With Wi-Fi off and mobile data and data roaming on, try loading a webpage in your browser. Does it load?

If it does, try sending a picture message and let me know whether it goes through."}

{"message":"I’ve added 2 GB to 555-123-2002 for $4 and verified that the extra data is on your line. Your billing cycle ends February 28.

With Wi-Fi off and mobile data and data roaming on, try loading a webpage again. If it loads, try sending a picture message.

Please let me know whether each one works."}

{"message":"Yes—a missing MMSC URL prevents MMS from working. It tells your phone where to send picture messages.

Please reset your APN settings to their defaults using reset_apn_settings, then restart your phone so the changes take effect.

After restarting, check that mobile data and data roaming are on, then try sending a picture message again. Does it go through?"}

{"message":"Yes, there are a few more checks we can try. First, please check your APN settings again: is the MMSC URL now present?

Also, check whether Wi-Fi Calling is on. If it is, turn it off and try sending the picture message again—it can sometimes interfere with MMS.

Please tell me what you find and whether the message sends."}

{"message":"There’s one permission still to check: Phone permission is not the same as SMS permission.

Please check whether your messaging app has SMS permission. If it’s missing, grant it and try sending the picture again.

Also, what network is your phone actually connected to—2G, 3G, 4G/LTE, or 5G? The preferred mode doesn’t confirm the current connection. If SMS permission is already granted and you’re connected to 3G or higher, let me know so I can arrange further help."}

